## Lore

The Seng Ren lived a life of quiet, wholly inward meditation, each day a little closer to erasing the world and slipping past its illusions. Then, in the middle of that hard-won nothingness, a tiny glimmer of light appeared, and kept appearing, day after day. The abbot warned against touching it. The monk touched it anyway.

What poured out was chi-gung: an endless geyser of inner energy, a fountain of I.S.P. that made further meditation impossible for everyone nearby. Expelled from the monastery, the monk was sent out into a world of mortals, the dead and damned and the Hells of the Yama Kings, with the abbot''s charge to stop helping only himself and to do some good with the gift.

Chi-gung turns what would otherwise be psionics into a wide range of uses of the body''s own internal power, the mystic martial arts counterpart of a Mind Melter. Every Seng Ren shares the same background: years of withdrawal and austerity begun as a child or teenager, never later than the early twenties, followed by the shock of the outside world once the powers awaken. Uncertain how to proceed and wary of how their power might be misused, they question new friends closely, and anyone who seeks to lead them more closely still. Monasteries in Rifts China have accepted non-humans for over a century, so any race drawn to meditation can become one, and the order is evenly split between men and women.

Standard equipment: up to four traditional Chinese weapons (3D6 rounds for each projectile weapon); sturdy traveling clothes with boots, hat, gloves and winter over-garments; simple temple garb of robe, warm cloak, sandals and begging bowl; a suit of embroidered silk indoor clothing with scarf and hat; identification documents including a passport from the monastery''s religious authorities and letters of recommendation, one from the abbot; a blank book with pen or pencil, 4D6+4 sheets of paper, solid ink, an ink block and bamboo brushes; several packages of tea and a collection of herbs; a tea bottle, kettle, two sets of chopsticks, a cooking knife and 10 cups of uncooked rice; a large shoulder bag, a belt pouch and one bamboo canteen of water.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'chi-gung-seng-ren');


